IMPORT prelude

-- Customs Tariff Act 1995 (Commonwealth of Australia) -- what the traveller or small importer meets: how the rate of duty
-- on goods is found (ss 6, 7, 9, 10, 14, 15, 16(1), (5)); a handful of representative Schedule 3 lines; the Schedule 4
-- concessional items a private importer meets (items 17, 18, 24, 25, 26, 28, 36, 37) and when one applies (ss 8, 18(1));
-- and the duty on an ad valorem rate (s 9).
--
-- SEAT: the traveller or small importer -- a person bringing goods into Australia, or ordering them from overseas. "What
-- rate of duty applies to my goods, given where they come from? Does a concession make them free? How much duty is that?"
-- The Comptroller-General's and the Collector's decisions (value of a part, s 9(2); being satisfied, Schedule 4 item 24)
-- are asked as facts.
--
-- Edition: Compilation No. 100, compilation date 4 July 2026 (includes amendments up to Act No. 67, 2026; register id
-- C2026C00399, 7 volumes), as deposited at ../../registers/source-bundle/C2004A04997.txt. Schedule 3 at lines 1134-30111,
-- Schedule 4 at 30112-30499.
--
-- READ WITH the Customs Act 1901 baseline (../../../customs-act-1901/encodings/baseline-2026-10/), which answers the
-- incoming passenger's questions -- the passenger concession (this Act's Schedule 4 item 15 with s 18), the incoming
-- passenger card, prohibited imports and the penalties -- and is not restated here: item 15 is left out of this module.
-- Cross-folder IMPORT is not possible in the stable l4, so the link is in NOTES.md.
--
-- SCHEDULE 3 IS NOT ENCODED WHOLE. It runs to some 29,000 lines of tariff classifications. Nine representative lines a
-- traveller or small importer meets are encoded with their rates as printed; any other classification answers
-- `not covered`, and its rates are the person's to look up. Classification itself (the Interpretation Rules in Schedule 2,
-- s 7) is a judgment, asked as a fact: which line the goods fall under.
--
-- NOT ENCODED. The preferences for Papua New Guinea, Forum Island, Developing and Least Developed Countries (s 16(1)(c),
-- (e)-(i); the countries are in the regulations, not deposited); the free trade agreement schedules' tables (Schedules 4A
-- to 16): whether a schedule lists the classification, and its rate, is asked as a fact; ss 16A-16B (safeguards), 17
-- (constituents), 18A-18B (Russia, Belarus, Ukraine), 19-19AD (excise-equivalent indexation: alcohol, tobacco, fuel), 20;
-- Schedule 4 items other than 17, 18, 24, 25, 26, 28, 36, 37; the by-laws (not deposited: whether goods are "as prescribed by
-- by-law" is asked); the value of goods (the Customs Act's).
--
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Written from the importer's seat with the 2026-10-18 defaults. No pipeline, no coverage table, no
-- independent test pass, no human gate. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Customs Tariff Act 1995 -- the traveller or small importer`

§§ `Questions this encoding does not cover`

DECLARE `Why not covered` IS ONE OF
    -- Schedule 3: only nine representative lines
    `only nine representative Schedule 3 classifications are encoded; look up the rate for this classification in Schedule 3`
    -- s 16(1)(c), (e)-(i)
    `the preferences for Papua New Guinea, Forum Island, Developing and Least Developed Countries are not encoded (their countries are listed in regulations not deposited)`
    -- Schedule 4 item 15
    `the passenger concession (Schedule 4 item 15) is answered by the Customs Act 1901 baseline, not restated here`

GIVEN a IS A TYPE
GIVETH A TYPE
DECLARE `Answer` a IS ONE OF
    `answered`    HAS `the answer` IS AN a
    `not covered` HAS `because`    IS A `Why not covered`

§§ `Schedule 3 -- representative tariff classifications`

-- s 6: the tariff classification is "the heading or subheading (a) in whose third column a rate of duty is set out; and (b)
-- under which the goods are classified". s 7(1): "The Interpretation Rules must be used for working out the tariff
-- classification". s 9(1): a percentage in a rate column is that percentage "of the value of the goods". s 10(1): "Free" is
-- a rate of duty. s 14(1)(a), (e)-(g), (i): "NZ", "DCS", "DCT", "CA" mark rates for New Zealand, Developing Countries and
-- Canada. The lines below, with their rates as printed in the third column (the deposit's line number in brackets):
--   8471.30.00 portable automatic data processing machines (a laptop), not more than 10 kg: Free [24638]
--   8517.13.00 smartphones: Free [25871]
--   6109.10.00 T-shirts, singlets and other vests, knitted or crocheted, of cotton: 5% [17358]
--   4202.21.00 handbags with outer surface of leather or composition leather: 5% [12025]
--   9503.00.20 dolls representing only human beings, and their parts and accessories: 5% CA:Free [29555]
--   8711.60.00 motorcycles (including mopeds) and cycles with an electric motor for propulsion: 5% DCS:4% DCT:5% [27836]
--   8703.80.11 passenger motor vehicles with only an electric motor, used or second-hand: 5% CA:Free [27330]
--   8703.80.12 the same, new, NSA, valued below the fuel-efficient car limit (Additional Note 6 to Chapter 87): Free [27333]
--   8703.80.19 the same, other: 5% CA:Free [27336]
DECLARE `Tariff classification` IS ONE OF
    `8471.30.00 a laptop or other portable computer of 10 kg or less`
    `8517.13.00 a smartphone`
    `6109.10.00 a cotton T-shirt, singlet or vest, knitted or crocheted`
    `4202.21.00 a leather handbag`
    `9503.00.20 a doll representing a human being, or its parts and accessories`
    `8711.60.00 an electric bicycle, scooter or motorcycle`
    `8703.80.11 a used electric passenger car`
    `8703.80.12 a new electric passenger car valued below the fuel-efficient car limit`
    `8703.80.19 another electric passenger car`
    `another classification`

-- The general rate, as a percentage of the value (0 for Free).
GIVEN `classification` IS A `Tariff classification`
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`the general rate in Schedule 3 for` MEANS
    CONSIDER `classification`
    WHEN `8471.30.00 a laptop or other portable computer of 10 kg or less` THEN JUST 0
    WHEN `8517.13.00 a smartphone` THEN JUST 0
    WHEN `8703.80.12 a new electric passenger car valued below the fuel-efficient car limit` THEN JUST 0
    WHEN `another classification` THEN NOTHING
    OTHERWISE JUST 5

-- The rate "that applies in relation to" Canada ("CA"), where the line sets one (s 14(1)(i)).
GIVEN `classification` IS A `Tariff classification`
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`the Canadian rate in Schedule 3 for` MEANS
    CONSIDER `classification`
    WHEN `9503.00.20 a doll representing a human being, or its parts and accessories` THEN JUST 0
    WHEN `8703.80.11 a used electric passenger car` THEN JUST 0
    WHEN `8703.80.19 another electric passenger car` THEN JUST 0
    OTHERWISE NOTHING

@export What is the general rate of customs duty in Schedule 3 for my goods (as a percentage of their value)?
GIVEN `the tariff classification` IS A `Tariff classification` @desc The Schedule 3 heading or subheading your goods fall under (worked out with the Interpretation Rules)
GIVETH AN `Answer` NUMBER
`The general rate for my goods` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the general rate in Schedule 3 for` `the tariff classification`
    WHEN JUST r THEN `answered` r
    WHEN NOTHING THEN `not covered` `only nine representative Schedule 3 classifications are encoded; look up the rate for this classification in Schedule 3`

§§ `s 16 -- which rate applies, given where the goods come from`

-- s 16(1)(a): goods that are no country's preference or originating goods: "by reference to the general rate". (b): New
-- Zealand originating goods: the NZ rate if one is set, "otherwise--Free". (d): the produce or manufacture of Canada: the CA
-- rate if set, "otherwise--by reference to the general rate". (ja)-(v): Singaporean, US, Thai, Peruvian, Chilean, AANZ,
-- Pacific Islands, TPP, Malaysian, Indonesian, Korean, Indian, Japanese, Chinese, Hong Kong, RCEP, UK or UAE originating
-- goods: "if the goods are classified to a heading or subheading in Schedule 3 that is specified in column 2 of an item in the
-- table in Schedule [4A to 16]--by reference to the rate of duty set out in column 3 of that item; or (ii) otherwise--Free".
-- (5): where more than one paragraph applies, "the paragraph in respect of which the least amount of duty would be payable".
-- Whether goods are "originating goods" is decided under the Customs Act 1901 (asked as a fact). None of the nine lines sets
-- an NZ rate.
DECLARE `Where my goods come from` IS ONE OF
    `New Zealand originating goods`
    `the produce or manufacture of Canada`
    `originating goods of a free trade agreement partner (Singapore, US, Thailand, Peru, Chile, ASEAN-Australia-NZ, Pacific Islands, TPP, Malaysia, Indonesia, Korea, India, Japan, China, Hong Kong, RCEP, UK or UAE)`
    `the produce of Papua New Guinea, a Forum Island Country, or a Developing or Least Developed Country`
    `none of these (no preference)`

GIVEN `general` IS A NUMBER
      `origin` IS A `Where my goods come from`
      `canadian` IS A MAYBE NUMBER
      `the FTA schedule lists it` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the FTA rate` IS A NUMBER
      `also canadian` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH AN `Answer` NUMBER
`the s 16 rate` MEANS
    CONSIDER `origin`
    WHEN `New Zealand originating goods` THEN `answered` 0
    WHEN `the produce or manufacture of Canada` THEN `answered` (`the Canadian rate or the general` `canadian` `general`)
    WHEN `originating goods of a free trade agreement partner (Singapore, US, Thailand, Peru, Chile, ASEAN-Australia-NZ, Pacific Islands, TPP, Malaysia, Indonesia, Korea, India, Japan, China, Hong Kong, RCEP, UK or UAE)` THEN
        IF `also canadian`
        THEN `answered` (`the lesser of` (`the FTA rate or Free` `the FTA schedule lists it` `the FTA rate`) (`the Canadian rate or the general` `canadian` `general`))
        ELSE `answered` (`the FTA rate or Free` `the FTA schedule lists it` `the FTA rate`)
    WHEN `the produce of Papua New Guinea, a Forum Island Country, or a Developing or Least Developed Country` THEN `not covered` `the preferences for Papua New Guinea, Forum Island, Developing and Least Developed Countries are not encoded (their countries are listed in regulations not deposited)`
    WHEN `none of these (no preference)` THEN `answered` `general`

GIVEN `canadian` IS A MAYBE NUMBER
      `general` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the Canadian rate or the general` MEANS
    CONSIDER `canadian`
    WHEN JUST r THEN r
    WHEN NOTHING THEN `general`

GIVEN `listed` IS A BOOLEAN
      `rate` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the FTA rate or Free` MEANS IF `listed` THEN `rate` ELSE 0

GIVEN `one` IS A NUMBER
      `other` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the lesser of` MEANS IF `one` LESS THAN `other` THEN `one` ELSE `other`

@export What rate of customs duty applies to my goods, given where they come from (as a percentage of their value)?
GIVEN `the tariff classification` IS A `Tariff classification` @desc The Schedule 3 heading or subheading your goods fall under
      `where my goods come from` IS A `Where my goods come from` @desc Whether the goods are originating goods of New Zealand or of a free trade agreement partner, or the produce or manufacture of Canada (under the Customs Act's rules of origin), or none of these
      `the agreement's schedule lists this classification` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a free trade agreement partner's originating goods: whether that agreement's schedule in this Act (Schedules 4A to 16) lists this heading or subheading with a rate
      `the rate it lists` IS A NUMBER @desc The rate that schedule lists, as a percentage (0 for Free); 0 if it lists none
      `the goods are also the produce of Canada` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a free trade agreement partner's goods: whether they are also the produce or manufacture of Canada (in which case the lower rate applies, s 16(5))
GIVETH AN `Answer` NUMBER
`The rate for my goods by origin` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the general rate in Schedule 3 for` `the tariff classification`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN `not covered` `only nine representative Schedule 3 classifications are encoded; look up the rate for this classification in Schedule 3`
    WHEN JUST general THEN `the s 16 rate` general `where my goods come from` (`the Canadian rate in Schedule 3 for` `the tariff classification`) `the agreement's schedule lists this classification` `the rate it lists` `the goods are also the produce of Canada`

§§ `Schedule 4, ss 8 and 18 -- does a concession make my goods free`

-- Schedule 4, the items a private importer meets (the deposit's line number in brackets):
--   17 goods, as prescribed by by-law, exported from Australia and returned without any treatment, repair, renovation,
--      alteration or other process since (and not item 17A goods): Free [30214]
--   18 goods returned after repair overseas free of charge under warranty, or supplied free under warranty or a global
--      product safety recall to replace goods previously imported, not upgraded and without drawback: Free [30232]
--   24 goods not to be sold or used for trade that the Collector is satisfied became the importer's under a will or
--      intestacy while the importer was resident in Australia: Free [30285]
--   25 goods, as prescribed by by-law, that are trophies won, or decorations, medallions or certificates awarded, outside
--      Australia: Free [30294]
--   26 goods, as prescribed by by-law, "whose value is less than the amount prescribed by by-law": Free [30304]
--   28 aids and appliances, as prescribed by by-law, for persons with disabilities: Free [30314]
--   36 vehicles aged 30 years or more that are passenger motor vehicles (or certain utilities or pick-ups): Free [30351]
--   37 used or second-hand passenger motor vehicles, as prescribed by by-law: 5% (CA: Free) [30356]
-- s 8(1): an item applies to goods "described in the second column". s 18(1): an item that prima facie applies "only applies
-- to those goods if the duty payable ... under that item is less than the duty that ... would be payable" under Schedule 3 or
-- the free trade schedule that applies. The by-laws are not deposited: whether goods are "as prescribed by by-law" is asked.
-- Every item here is Free or an ad valorem rate, so comparing rates compares duty on the same value.
DECLARE `The concessional item` IS ONE OF
    `item 17: goods I exported and brought back unaltered`
    `item 18: goods repaired or replaced free under warranty or a safety recall`
    `item 24: goods I inherited, not for sale or trade`
    `item 25: a trophy, decoration, medallion or certificate won or awarded overseas`
    `item 26: goods of low value`
    `item 28: an aid or appliance for a person with a disability`
    `item 36: a passenger car, or certain utilities, 30 or more years old`
    `item 37: a used or second-hand passenger car`
    `item 15: goods brought in as a passenger`

DECLARE `Does the concession apply` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the item applies, and the goods are Free (ss 8, 18(1), Schedule 4)`
    `yes: item 37 applies, at 5% of the value (ss 8, 18(1), Schedule 4)`
    `no: the item gives no less duty than the rate that otherwise applies, so it does not apply and that rate stands (s 18(1))`
    `no: the goods are not described in the item (or not prescribed by the by-law it needs) (s 8(1))`

-- Item 37's rate "5% ... CA:Free" (s 14(1)(i)): Free for the produce of Canada.
GIVEN `item` IS A `The concessional item`
      `from Canada` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the item's rate` MEANS
    CONSIDER `item`
    WHEN `item 37: a used or second-hand passenger car` THEN IF `from Canada` THEN 0 ELSE 5
    OTHERWISE 0

@export Does a Schedule 4 concession make my goods free, or cheaper?
GIVEN `the concessional item` IS A `The concessional item` @desc Which concessional item in Schedule 4 might cover your goods
      `my goods are described in the item` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your goods fit the item's description (and, for items 17, 25, 26, 28 and 37, are of the kind the Customs By-laws prescribe; for item 24, the Collector is satisfied you inherited them while resident in Australia)
      `the rate that otherwise applies` IS A NUMBER @desc The rate of duty that would otherwise apply to your goods under Schedule 3 or the free trade schedule that applies, as a percentage (0 for Free)
      `the goods are the produce of Canada` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the goods are the produce or manufacture of Canada (item 37 is Free for them)
GIVETH AN `Answer` `Does the concession apply`
`Does a concession apply to my goods` MEANS
    IF `the concessional item` EQUALS `item 15: goods brought in as a passenger`
    THEN `not covered` `the passenger concession (Schedule 4 item 15) is answered by the Customs Act 1901 baseline, not restated here`
    ELSE IF (NOT `my goods are described in the item`)
         THEN `answered` `no: the goods are not described in the item (or not prescribed by the by-law it needs) (s 8(1))`
         ELSE IF (`the item's rate` `the concessional item` `the goods are the produce of Canada`) AT LEAST `the rate that otherwise applies`
              THEN `answered` `no: the item gives no less duty than the rate that otherwise applies, so it does not apply and that rate stands (s 18(1))`
              ELSE IF `the concessional item` EQUALS `item 37: a used or second-hand passenger car` AND (NOT `the goods are the produce of Canada`)
                   THEN `answered` `yes: item 37 applies, at 5% of the value (ss 8, 18(1), Schedule 4)`
                   ELSE `answered` `yes: the item applies, and the goods are Free (ss 8, 18(1), Schedule 4)`

§§ `s 9 -- how much duty`

-- s 9(1): a percentage in a rate column "is to that percentage of the value of the goods". The value is worked out under the
-- Customs Act 1901 (s 159), asked as a fact. GST and any excise-equivalent duty are other law.
@export How much customs duty is payable on my goods at an ad valorem rate?
GIVEN `the rate as a percentage` IS A NUMBER @desc The rate of duty that applies, as a percentage of value (0 for Free)
      `the customs value of the goods` IS A NUMBER @desc The value of the goods for customs purposes, in Australian dollars, as worked out under the Customs Act 1901
GIVETH A NUMBER
`The duty on my goods` MEANS `the customs value of the goods` TIMES `the rate as a percentage` DIVIDED BY 100
